--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,183 @@
+# Helpdesk API
+
+Rails API-only backend backed by PostgreSQL. Set `DATABASE_URL` to the connection URL for the environment you're running, then:
+
+```sh
+bundle install
+bin/rails db:prepare
+bin/rails server
+```
+
+`HELPDESK_FRONTEND_URL` defaults to `http://localhost:3001`. Its origin is used for CORS and for the email links. `HELPDESK_MAIL_FROM` defaults to `support@example.test`.
+
+Development email is written to `tmp/mails/`. Tests use Action Mailer's in-memory deliveries. In production, set `SECRET_KEY_BASE` and configure `SMTP_HOST`, `SMTP_PORT`, and, if required, `SMTP_USERNAME` and `SMTP_PASSWORD`. The production server is configured for a TLS-terminating reverse proxy; apply rate limits there for signups, sign-ins, and email link requests.
+
+## API
+
+All endpoints are under `/api/v1`. Send JSON for requests with a body and set `X-Helpdesk-Account` to the business subdomain on every request except `POST /account/create`. For authenticated requests, also set `Authorization: Bearer <session.token>`.
+
+| Method and path | JSON body | Success |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `POST /account/create` | `{"account":{"name":"Acme","subdomain":"acme","admin":{"name":"Jo","email":"jo@acme.test","password":"my-first-password"}}}` | `201` with `account` and `user`; sends a verification link |
+| `POST /registrations` | `{"user":{"name":"Avery","email":"avery@example.test","password":"my-customer-password"}}` | `201` with `user`; sends a verification link |
+| `GET /portal` | — | `200` with the business `name` and `subdomain` under `portal`; public |
+| `GET /account` | — | `200` with `account`; authenticated |
+| `POST /sessions` | `{"session":{"email":"jo@acme.test","password":"my-first-password"}}` | `200` with `account`, `user`, and `session.token`; requires a verified email |
+| `DELETE /session` | — | `204`; revokes the supplied session |
+| `GET /me` | — | `200` with `user` |
+| `PATCH /me` | `{"user":{"name":"Jo Smith"}}` | `200` with updated `user` |
+| `PUT /me/password` | `{"password_change":{"current_password":"my-first-password","password":"my-new-password"}}` | `204`; revokes all of the user's sessions |
+| `POST /email_verifications` | `{"email_verification":{"email":"jo@acme.test"}}` | `202` whether or not there is an eligible recipient |
+| `POST /email_verifications/confirm` | `{"email_verification":{"token":"TOKEN"}}` | `200` with verified `user` |
+| `POST /password_resets` | `{"password_reset":{"email":"jo@acme.test"}}` | `202` whether or not there is an eligible recipient |
+| `POST /password_resets/confirm` | `{"password_reset":{"token":"TOKEN","password":"my-new-password"}}` | `200` with `user`; revokes all of the user's sessions |
+| `POST /account/cancel` | — | `200` with `account`; owner only |
+| `POST /account/reactivate` | — | `200` with `account`; owner only |
+
+Subdomains and emails are normalized to lowercase. Emails are unique per business, not globally. Passwords must be at least 8 characters and at most 72 bytes. Sessions expire after 30 days. Verification and reset messages link to `/verify-email?account=SUBDOMAIN&token=TOKEN` and `/reset-password?account=SUBDOMAIN&token=TOKEN`, respectively. Links are single-use, expire after 24 hours (verification) or one hour (reset), and are replaced whenever a new link of the same kind is issued.
+
+After cancellation, the portal is unavailable and existing sessions are revoked. The owner can recover their password if needed, sign in again, and reactivate within 90 days. The new session cannot use ordinary business endpoints until reactivation. Schedule this task regularly to permanently delete overdue accounts and their users, sessions, links, invitations, tickets, and comments:
+
+```sh
+bin/rails helpdesk:purge_cancelled_accounts
+```
+
+## Invitations and staff
+
+Admins can manage people in their business. An invitation is for an `admin` or `agent` and reserves its email against customer registration until it expires, is revoked, or is accepted. Emails already registered in the same business cannot be invited.
+
+| Method and path | JSON body | Success |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `POST /invitations` | `{"invitation":{"email":"sam@example.test","role":"agent"}}` | `201` with `invitation`; sends an invitation email |
+| `GET /invitations` | — | `200` with `invitations`; filter by `?status=pending`, `accepted`, `revoked`, or `expired` |
+| `GET /invitations/:id` | — | `200` with `invitation` |
+| `POST /invitations/:id/resend` | — | `200` with `invitation`; replaces the token and expiry on a pending or expired invitation if the address is still available |
+| `DELETE /invitations/:id` | — | `204`; revokes a pending invitation |
+| `POST /invitation_previews` | `{"invitation_preview":{"token":"TOKEN"}}` | `200` with `invitation_preview` (`email`, `role`, `account_name`, `account_subdomain`, `expires_at`); no sign-in needed |
+| `POST /invitation_acceptances` | `{"invitation_acceptance":{"token":"TOKEN","name":"Sam","password":"my-team-password"}}` | `201` with `user` and `account`; no sign-in needed |
+| `GET /users` | — | `200` with `users`; filter with `?role=agent` and/or `?suspended=true` or `false` |
+| `GET /users/:id` | — | `200` with `user` |
+| `PATCH /users/:id` | `{"user":{"suspended":true}}` | `200` with updated `user`; use `false` to restore access |
+| `GET /staff` | — | `200` with `staff` entries (`id`, `name`, `role`) for active admins and agents; available to staff |
+
+Invitation messages link to `/accept-invitation?account=SUBDOMAIN&token=TOKEN` on the frontend. Use the `account` value for `X-Helpdesk-Account` on both preview and acceptance. Tokens are single-use and expire after seven days. Accepting verifies the email immediately, so the new user can sign in without a separate verification message. Invitation, user, and staff lists are ordered by ID ascending.
+
+Every `user` includes a `suspended` boolean. Suspension revokes existing sessions, prevents signing in, and unassigns the user's tickets. Restoring access does not restore the old sessions or assignments. An admin cannot suspend the last active admin.
+
+## Staff groups
+
+Groups organize tickets but do not restrict access: admins and agents can browse all groups, memberships, and tickets in their account. Only admins can change groups and memberships.
+
+| Method and path | JSON body | Success |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `GET /groups` | — | `200` with `groups` |
+| `GET /groups/:id` | — | `200` with `group` |
+| `POST /groups` | `{"group":{"name":"Billing"}}` | `201` with `group` |
+| `PATCH /groups/:id` | `{"group":{"name":"Invoices"}}` | `200` with `group` |
+| `DELETE /groups/:id` | — | `204` |
+| `GET /group_memberships` | — | `200` with `group_memberships` |
+| `GET /group_memberships/:id` | — | `200` with `group_membership` |
+| `POST /group_memberships` | `{"group_membership":{"group_id":20,"user_id":12,"default":true}}` | `201` with `group_membership` |
+| `PUT /group_memberships/:id/make_default` | — | `200` with `group_membership` |
+| `DELETE /group_memberships/:id` | — | `204` |
+
+Both lists are ordered by ascending `id`. The membership list supports `group_id` and `user_id` filters. Staff can belong to multiple groups, with one membership per group. The first membership becomes the default automatically; use `default: true` on creation or `make_default` to change it. When a default membership is deleted, the remaining membership with the lowest ID becomes the default. Suspension preserves memberships and defaults.
+
+## Portal conversations
+
+Customers register as members of the business in `X-Helpdesk-Account`, then use the same email verification, sign-in, profile, and password recovery endpoints as staff. Customers can only see their own requests. Staff (admins and agents) can work on all tickets in their business.
+
+| Method and path | JSON body | Success |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `POST /requests` | `{"request":{"subject":"Missing receipt","comment":{"body":"I cannot download it."}}}` | `201` with the new, open `request` |
+| `GET /requests` or `GET /tickets` | — | `200` with a page of `requests` or `tickets` |
+| `GET /requests/:id` or `GET /tickets/:id` | — | `200` with `request` or `ticket` |
+| `GET /requests/:id/comments` or `GET /tickets/:id/comments` | — | `200` with a page of `comments` |
+| `PATCH /requests/:id` | `{"request":{"comment":{"body":"Thanks!"},"status":"solved"}}` | `200` with updated `request` |
+| `PATCH /tickets/:id` | `{"ticket":{"comment":{"body":"Please try again now."},"status":"pending","priority":"high"}}` | `200` with updated `ticket` |
+| `POST /tickets/:id/merge` | `{"merge":{"target_id":57}}` | `200` with `source` and `target` tickets; staff only |
+
+Requests and tickets include `id`, `subject`, `status`, `requester_id`, `merged_into_id` (`null` unless merged), `created_at`, and `updated_at`. Tickets also include `priority` (`normal` by default, or `high`), `requester` with `id`, `name`, and `email`, plus `assignee_id` and `assignee`, and `group_id` and `group`. The nested `assignee` and `group` each contain `id` and `name`, or are `null` when not set. Comments include `id`, `body`, `author` (with `id` and `name`), `public`, and `created_at`.
+
+A PATCH can include a comment, a status, or, for tickets, `assignee_id`, `group_id`, and `priority`. Assigning an active staff member to an ungrouped ticket selects their default group if they have one. An explicit assignment to someone outside the ticket's group is rejected. Moving a ticket to a different group clears its existing assignee unless they belong to the new group. Setting `assignee_id` to `null` leaves the group in place; setting `group_id` to `null` clears both. Deleting a group clears both fields on its tickets, while removing a membership clears that staff member's assignments in that group without moving the tickets.
+
+A customer can set `solved`; a reply without that status reopens a pending or solved request. Staff can set `open`, `pending`, or `solved`; a staff reply without a status leaves the ticket's status unchanged. Comments are public by default. Staff can set `comment.public` to `false` for a private note. Assignment and group changes, priority changes, and private notes do not change the request’s customer-facing `updated_at` or its position in the request list; public replies and status changes do. Public staff replies send email only for tickets opened by email.
+
+Staff can combine inbox filters, for example `GET /api/v1/tickets?status=open&assignee_id=unassigned&priority=high&q=receipt`:
+
+| Query parameter | Matches |
+| --- | --- |
+| `status` | `open`, `pending`, `solved`, or `closed` |
+| `assignee_id` | A positive assignee ID, or `unassigned` for tickets without an assignee |
+| `group_id` | A positive group ID, or `unassigned` for tickets without a group |
+| `requester_id` | A positive requester ID |
+| `priority` | `normal` or `high` |
+| `q` | A literal, case-insensitive substring of the subject or any public comment. An empty search is ignored. |
+
+Customers can use `status` on `GET /api/v1/requests` to filter their own requests. Other inbox filters are staff-only and ignored on the request list. Priority, group, and assignment fields are staff-only. Invalid values for supported filters return `400`.
+
+Request, ticket, and comment lists take positive integers `page` (default `1`) and `per_page` (default `25`, maximum `100`). Alongside the records, they return the total matching `count`, `page`, `per_page`, `next_page`, and `previous_page` (`null` when there is no next or previous page). Requests and tickets are ordered by latest activity first; comments are ordered oldest first.
+
+### Merging duplicate tickets
+
+Staff can merge two different tickets belonging to the same customer in the same business. Neither ticket may already be closed. The source becomes `closed`, and `merged_into_id` points to the target. Staff and the customer can still read the source and its existing comments through their usual endpoints. The merge does not move comments or change the target’s status, assignment, or reply address. If the target is later merged into a third ticket, the original source still points to the original target.
+
+For example, `POST /api/v1/tickets/42/merge` can take this body:
+
+```json
+{
+  "merge": {
+    "target_id": 57,
+    "source_comment": { "body": "Continuing in ticket 57.", "public": true },
+    "target_comment": { "body": "Duplicate checked.", "public": false }
+  }
+}
+```
+
+Both notes get default text if no `body` is supplied and are private unless `public` is `true`. Merge notes never send email, even when public. A merge applies both notes and the link together or makes no changes. An out-of-account or missing ticket returns `404`; an ineligible merge returns `422`.
+
+Only a merge sets `closed` through the API. Closed tickets reject edits and incoming email replies; email sent to a closed ticket’s reply address is not forwarded to the target. Assignment cleanup on staff suspension, membership removal, or group deletion still applies.
+
+## Collection pagination
+
+All collection endpoints support cursor pagination: `tickets`, `requests`, both comment lists, `users`, `staff`, `groups`, `group_memberships`, and `invitations`. Start with a nested `page[size]`, for example:
+
+```http
+GET /api/v1/tickets?page[size]=25&status=open
+```
+
+Cursor responses include the same record array plus `meta` and `links`:
+
+```json
+{"tickets":[],"meta":{"has_more":false,"after_cursor":null,"before_cursor":null},"links":{"next":null,"prev":null}}
+```
+
+On a nonempty page, `after_cursor` marks the last record and `before_cursor` marks the first. Follow `links.next` or `links.prev` (relative URLs that retain the filters and effective size), or pass the corresponding cursor as `page[after]` or `page[before]`. Use the same account, signed-in user, endpoint, and filters. `meta.has_more` means there are more results **in the direction of the request**: forward by default, backward for `page[before]`. Links are `null` when unavailable. The page size defaults to 25 and is capped at 100. Invalid cursors and combining `page[after]` with `page[before]` return `400`.
+
+Cursor pagination uses the same ordering as the original lists, breaking timestamp ties by ID. The cursor retains its original position even if the boundary record moves or is deleted. This is a live view, not a snapshot: updates that change a record’s sort position or whether it matches a filter can change which page it appears on.
+
+Scalar `page` and `per_page` still use page numbers. Without pagination parameters, tickets, requests, and comment lists continue to return a numbered first page; the other collections still return their unpaged lists. Do not combine `per_page` with nested `page` parameters.
+
+## Support by email
+
+Set `HELPDESK_SUPPORT_DOMAIN` to the domain that receives support mail (default: `support.example.test`). A business with subdomain `acme` receives new requests at `acme@support.example.test`. Configure a trusted SMTP relay for the domain to POST the original RFC 822 message to `/rails/action_mailbox/relay/inbound_emails` over HTTPS. Preserve the recipient headers (including `Cc`) and enforce your sender-authentication policy at the relay. Use HTTP Basic authentication with username `actionmailbox` and set the shared secret in `RAILS_INBOUND_EMAIL_PASSWORD`.
+
+```sh
+curl --fail-with-body --user "actionmailbox:$RAILS_INBOUND_EMAIL_PASSWORD" \
+  --header 'Content-Type: message/rfc822' --data-binary @message.eml \
+  https://api.example.test/rails/action_mailbox/relay/inbound_emails
+```
+
+A new message to a business's support address opens a ticket with its subject and plain-text body. A new customer receives a portal verification link; once verified, they can set a password using the password-reset flow. An incoming message does not verify an account. Public staff replies on email-origin tickets are sent to the requester with a private `Reply-To` at the support domain. The relay must accept these `reply+…` addresses too. Only mail from the requester to the private address can append a reply and reopen a pending or solved ticket. Sending to the plain support address alone starts a new ticket, even if the message has email threading headers.
+
+The relay acknowledges intake with `204`, including messages later ignored because they are ambiguous, automated, list mail, or otherwise invalid. Accepted messages are deduplicated by `Message-ID`; rejected deliveries may be retried. Tests process mail inline. For production, use persistent Active Storage for the raw messages and a durable Active Job backend that services the Action Mailbox routing and incineration queues.
+
+## Tests
+
+Use a dedicated scratch database. If `DATABASE_URL` is already set to your development database, pass `TEST_DATABASE_URL` to override it for the test environment:
+
+```sh
+export TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://localhost/helpdesk_test
+RAILS_ENV=test bin/rails db:prepare
+bin/rails test
+```
